from pathlib import Path
import json
import zipfile

OUT = Path(__file__).resolve().parent
STUDY = OUT.parents[1] / 'studies/glyph-profiles'
report = json.loads((OUT / 'verification.json').read_text(encoding='utf-8'))
assert report['passed'] and all(c['passed'] for c in report['checks'])
files = ['project.godot', 'study.tscn', 'study.gd', 'glyph_solid.gd', 'README.md',
         'JetBrainsMono-Medium.ttf', 'JetBrainsMono-OFL.txt', 'Roboto-Regular.ttf', 'OFL.txt']
with zipfile.ZipFile(OUT / 'glyph-profiles.zip', 'w', zipfile.ZIP_DEFLATED) as archive:
    for name in files:
        archive.write(STUDY / name, 'glyph-profiles/' + name)
with zipfile.ZipFile(OUT / 'glyph-profiles.zip') as archive:
    assert archive.testzip() is None
    for name in files:
        assert archive.read('glyph-profiles/' + name) == (STUDY / name).read_bytes()
page = '''<!doctype html><html lang="en"><meta charset="utf-8"><meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width,initial-scale=1">
<title>A letter becomes a body — Ada</title><style>
*{box-sizing:border-box}body{margin:0;background:#211e27;color:#e6ddcf;font:19px/1.65 Georgia,serif}main{max-width:1300px;margin:auto;padding:40px 25px 80px}header,.copy{max-width:830px;margin:35px auto 55px}h1{font-size:clamp(44px,7vw,79px);line-height:1.03;font-weight:400}h2{font-size:31px;font-weight:400}small,figcaption,nav,.note{font:14px/1.65 system-ui,sans-serif;color:#c6b4bd}a{color:#f2c2d0;text-underline-offset:4px}nav{display:flex;gap:25px;flex-wrap:wrap}figure{margin:30px 0}img{width:100%;display:block}figcaption{padding:12px 0}.button{display:inline-block;border:1px solid #b58a9c;padding:10px 20px;font:16px/1.5 system-ui,sans-serif;text-decoration:none}code{font:.84em Consolas,monospace}.path{border-left:2px solid #bd8b9e;padding-left:22px;font-size:24px}table{border-collapse:collapse;font:16px/1.5 system-ui,sans-serif;width:100%}td,th{padding:13px 8px;border-bottom:1px solid #52424c;text-align:left;vertical-align:top}a:focus-visible{outline:2px solid #f2c2d0;outline-offset:4px}@media(max-width:650px){main{padding:22px 15px}table{font-size:14px}}
</style><main><nav><a href="#study">The specimens</a><a href="#method">Where we intervene</a><a href="#fonts">Symbol fonts</a><a href="#make">Open the study</a></nav>
<header><small>ADA / ENGINE STUDY / 4 OCTOBER 2026</small><h1>A letter becomes<br>a body</h1><p>A font is already a collection of drawn shapes. Here we borrow five of them, change their points, and give them another use.</p></header>
<figure id="study"><a href="overview.png"><img src="overview.png" alt="Five native Godot sculptures: a letter B, a widened and sheared B, a deep O, an asterisk column and a twisted ampersand column."></a><figcaption>Native Godot capture. The font supplies the initial shapes; our geometry code constructs their depth.</figcaption></figure>
<section class="copy"><h2>Read it. Walk around it.</h2><p>B remains a letter after its outline leans and widens. O has an opening. Turn an asterisk onto the floor and draw its profile upward: it becomes a fluted column. Turn the ampersand gradually during extrusion and its outlines form a twisting body.</p><p>The experiments suggest a question for Ada: when does a character stop being read and start being used? Its earlier identity may remain visible even after its purpose changes.</p></section>
<figure><a href="outline-change.png"><img src="outline-change.png" alt="Original B and changed B, both with their two holes retained, beside a deep O."></a><figcaption>The pink B is changed in two dimensions before depth is constructed. This does not modify the font file.</figcaption></figure>
<section class="copy" id="method"><h2>Where we intervene</h2><p class="path">Glyph → flat face → move points → extrude → mesh</p><p>This prototype asks <code>TextMesh</code> for a flat, triangulated glyph. We identify its boundary edges, change the planar points and construct caps and walls. The O opening survives, including in the temporary physics test.</p><table><tr><th>Route</th><th>What it gives us</th></tr><tr><td>Built-in TextMesh</td><td>Font choice, a glyph, curve sampling and straight depth.</td></tr><tr><td>Changed flat face</td><td>The working shortcut shown here: reuse triangulation, alter the shape, then construct depth.</td></tr><tr><td>Raw Bézier contours</td><td>More freedom, with responsibility for sampling curves, preserving holes and triangulating the edited outline.</td></tr><tr><td>Finished mesh or shader</td><td>Bend a surface already made. A visual shader does not automatically update collision.</td></tr></table><p class="note">The shortcut is suitable for the tested shapes and gentle deformations. It does not solve arbitrary self-intersections or transitions between different numbers of holes. Raw contour points use their third component as a curve tag, not a Z coordinate.</p></section>
<figure><a href="profiles-above.png"><img src="profiles-above.png" alt="Elevated view exposing the asterisk cross-section and the twisting ampersand extrusion."></a><figcaption>The section becomes easier to recognize from above. Straight extrusion and a sequence of rotated sections produce different bodies.</figcaption></figure>
<section class="copy" id="fonts"><h2>A symbol can be a profile</h2><p>A Unicode star from the installed Segoe UI Symbol font successfully became a closed mesh. The font remains on the local computer; it is not bundled here. The downloadable sculptures use JetBrains Mono, with its license included.</p><p>“Any font” would be too strong. Godot needs usable vector outlines. Bitmap emoji will not work through TextMesh. The installed Wingdings file exposed no supported characters in this test, and a Roboto ampersand failed triangulation at the tested settings. A filename ending in <code>.ttf</code> does not certify the result.</p></section>
<figure><a href="dingbat.png"><img src="dingbat.png" alt="An extruded and sheared star from a real Unicode symbol font in the Godot study."></a><figcaption>U+2605 BLACK STAR from Segoe UI Symbol. This separate native test replaces O with the symbol.</figcaption></figure>
<section class="copy"><h2>Related profiles, different inputs</h2><p><code>TubeTrailMesh</code> samples a radius curve along a tube. A glyph can have an outer contour, several holes and disconnected pieces. Both can generate volume from a profile, but we must preserve the differences between their data.</p><p>Possible next encounters: an O large enough to enter, an asterisk column whose origin becomes visible from above, and one letter returning as a sign, a handle and a passage. These are development directions, not implemented interactions in this study.</p></section>
<section class="copy" id="make"><h2>Open the study</h2><a class="button" href="glyph-profiles.zip" download>Download the small Godot project</a><p>Open <code>project.godot</code> in Godot 4.6 and run. Drag to orbit; scroll to move nearer. All five bodies are created in code, with direct references.</p><p class="note">Desktop geometry experiment. No museum placement, VR locomotion or new course requirement. CHECK_COUNT native checks passed; physical headset use remains untested.</p><p><a href="verification.json">Native check results</a> · <a href="../../studies/glyph-profiles/README.md">Method and limits</a> · <a href="../../studies/glyph-profiles/glyph_solid.gd">Geometry source</a></p><p class="note">Engine references: <a href="https://docs.godotengine.org/en/4.6/classes/class_textmesh.html">TextMesh</a>, <a href="https://docs.godotengine.org/en/4.6/classes/class_textserver.html#class-textserver-method-font-get-glyph-contours">glyph contours</a>, <a href="https://github.com/godotengine/godot/blob/4.6/scene/resources/3d/primitive_meshes.cpp">implementation</a>, <a href="https://docs.godotengine.org/en/4.6/classes/class_tubetrailmesh.html">TubeTrailMesh</a>.</p></section></main></html>'''
(OUT / 'index.html').write_text(page.replace('CHECK_COUNT', str(len(report['checks']))), encoding='utf-8')
print(json.dumps({'project_files': len(files), 'native_checks': len(report['checks']), 'passed': report['passed']}))
